<div class="wrap prose"><p class="muted">Oct 30, 2026 · Data from runs Oct 25–30, 2026</p>
<h1 class="ph">Which MCP servers work in Claude Code vs Cursor?</h1>
<p class="answer">Short answer: if an MCP server connects in one, it connects in the other. Across six runs (Oct 25–30, 2026) we did 614 MCP installs in the two agents. On the Oct 30 run, 30 of 52 servers connected in both Claude Code and Cursor, 22 failed in both, and none passed in one but not the other.</p>
<h2>How we tested</h2>
<p>Each server gets its real install in a throwaway sandbox with an empty home folder and no accounts or API keys. In Claude Code we run <code>claude mcp add</code>, then <code>claude mcp list</code> has to say <b>Connected</b>. In Cursor we write <code>~/.cursor/mcp.json</code>, then <code>cursor-agent mcp list</code> has to say <b>ready</b> and list the tools. Both use the same command line for the server, so the agent is the only thing that changes. Full method: <a href="/how-we-test/">How we test</a>.</p>
<h2>The numbers</h2>
