<p>The small gaps on Oct 25 and 26 were one-night failures that passed again the next night: <a href="/skills/cyanheads-pubmed-mcp-server/">cyanheads PubMed</a> in Claude Code, and <a href="/skills/mcp-git/">Git</a> and <a href="/skills/putervision-vision-memory-mcp/">vision-memory</a> in Cursor. That's 3 flips in 614 installs. The other 49 servers gave the same result in both agents every night.</p>
<h2>Official reference servers vs npm packages</h2>
<p class="answer">All 7 official reference servers connected in both agents on Oct 30: Everything, Fetch, Filesystem, Git, Memory, Sequential Thinking and Time. Of the 45 community MCP packages from npm, 23 connected and 22 didn't.</p>
<p>Every one of the 22 failures was "Failed to connect" in both agents. Our sandbox has no keys and runs on Linux, so this list includes servers that need an account or token (for example <a href="/skills/postman-postman-mcp-server/">Postman</a>, <a href="/skills/taazkareem-clickup-mcp-server/">ClickUp</a> and <a href="/skills/zereight-mcp-gitlab/">GitLab</a>) and macOS-only ones (<a href="/skills/apple-notes-mcp/">Apple Notes</a>, <a href="/skills/safari-mcp/">Safari</a>). A fail here means "won't start with zero setup," not "broken." Each server page has the log line from our run.</p>
<h2>What this means for you</h2>
<ul><li><b>Switching between Claude Code and Cursor?</b> Your MCP servers will very likely come with you. Agent choice made no difference in our data.</li>
<li><b>A server fails in both?</b> It's almost always the server's setup (keys, arguments, OS), not your agent. Check its README for env vars.</li>
<li><b>One failure isn't a verdict.</b> 3 one-night flips over six nights is why we rerun nightly. See <a href="/guides/flaky-vs-broken/">flaky vs broken installs</a>.</li></ul>
<h2>What's next</h2>
<p>Starting with the Oct 31 run, the same MCP servers are also tested in Codex, Grok Build and Pi. A trial on Oct 30 with 3 servers (ClaimIDX, cyanheads Git and a weather server) passed in all 5 agents, 15 of 15. We'll publish the five-agent comparison once a few nights of data are in.</p>
